# Comparison of the formalized Milnor rings with the paper's definitions

Blueprint, Section 1.2 and the start of Section 7.

This file (with `Comparison/Integral.lean`, `Comparison/Rational.lean`, `Comparison/Relative.lean`)
is the link between the paper's definitions of the Milnor rings and the ones in terms of which
Theorems B, C, D are formalized.

## The paper's definitions

For a field `K` and a subfield `k ⊆ K` the paper defines

* `K^M_*(K)`, the quotient of the tensor algebra over `ℤ` of the abelian group `K^×` by the
  two-sided ideal generated by the elements `x ⊗ (1 - x)`, `x ≠ 0, 1`;
* `𝒦_*(K) = ℚ ⊗ K^M_*(K)`;
* `𝒦_*(K|k)`, the quotient of `𝒦_*(K)` by the two-sided ideal generated by `k^× ⊆ 𝒦_1(K)`;

all graded by tensor degree, with `τ_*` induced by a field embedding `τ` and `r^deg` the
multiplication by `r ^ n` in degree `n`.

## The formal definitions

The formalization (`MilnorAlgDep.Milnor.Defs`) defines, for a subgroup `H ≤ Lˣ`, the `ℚ`-algebra
`MilnorKQ H` as the quotient (`RingQuot`) of `TensorAlgebra ℚ (VOf H)`,
`VOf H = ℚ ⊗[ℤ] Additive (Lˣ ⧸ H)`, by the relations `ι [a] * ι [b] ~ 0` for units `a, b` with
`a + b = 1`; `𝒦_*(L)` is `MilnorKQ ⊥` and `𝒦_*(L|l)` is `MilnorKQ (constUnits l L)`. Its grading
is `MilnorKQ.grade` (`(range ι) ^ n`), `τ_*` is `MilnorKQ.map τ`, `r^deg` is
`MilnorKQ.degScale r`, and `φ₁` is `MilnorKQ.degOne φ`.

## The comparison

1. `MilnorK L` (`Comparison/Integral.lean`) is the paper's `K^M_*(L)`, literally: the `RingQuot`
   of `TensorAlgebra ℤ (Additive Lˣ)` by the relations `ι a * ι b ~ 0` for units `a + b = 1`.
   The pairs of units with `a + b = 1` are exactly the pairs `(x, 1 - x)` with `x ≠ 0, 1`
   (`MilnorK.symbol_one_sub_eq_zero`), and `RingQuot r` is the quotient by the two-sided ideal
   generated by the `x - y` with `r x y` (`ker_comp_mkRingHom_eq_span`; for `MilnorK` this is
   `MilnorK.ker_mkRingHom`, for `MilnorKQ` it is `MilnorKQ.ker_mkAlgHom`).
2. `MilnorK.ratEquiv L : ℚ ⊗[ℤ] MilnorK L ≃ₐ[ℚ] MilnorKQ (⊥ : Subgroup Lˣ)`
   (`Comparison/Rational.lean`): the paper's `𝒦_*(L) = ℚ ⊗ K^M_*(L)` is `MilnorKQ ⊥`. The
   isomorphism sends `1 ⊗ ι a` to `ι [a]` (`MilnorK.ratEquiv_one_tmul_ι`) and `1 ⊗ {a, b}` to
   `{a, b}` (`MilnorK.ratEquiv_one_tmul_symbol`); in degree one it is the identification
   `ℚ ⊗ L^× = VOf ⊥` (`VOf.botEquiv`, `MilnorK.ratEquiv_baseChange_ι`). It is graded
   (`MilnorK.grade_bot_eq_span`, `MilnorK.map_baseChange_grade`: it maps `ℚ ⊗ K^M_n(L)`, the
   `ℚ`-span of `1 ⊗ K^M_n(L)`, onto `𝒦_n(L) = MilnorKQ.grade ⊥ n`) and compatible with
   `τ_*` (`MilnorK.ratEquiv_comp_ratMap`) and `r^deg` (`MilnorK.degScale_ratEquiv_tmul`).
3. `MilnorK.quotEquiv H : RingQuot (MilnorK.ConstRel H) ≃ₐ[ℚ] MilnorKQ H`
   (`Comparison/Relative.lean`), where `MilnorK.ConstRel H` is the relation `1 ⊗ ι c ~ 0`,
   `c ∈ H`, on `ℚ ⊗[ℤ] MilnorK L`. For `H = constUnits l L` (the image of `lˣ`) the left hand
   side is the paper's `𝒦_*(L|l)`: the quotient of `ℚ ⊗ K^M_*(L)` by the two-sided ideal generated
   by `l^× ⊆ 𝒦_1(L)` (`MilnorK.relQuotEquiv l L`). Equivalently
   (`MilnorK.ker_proj_comp_ratEquiv`), the kernel of `ℚ ⊗[ℤ] MilnorK L → MilnorKQ H` is
   `TwoSidedIdeal.span {1 ⊗ ι c | c ∈ H}`. The isomorphism is
   compatible with degree one (`MilnorK.quotEquiv_mk_one_tmul_ι`), symbols, the grading
   (`MilnorK.grade_eq_span`), `τ_*` (`MilnorK.quotEquiv_mk_ratMap`) and `r^deg`
   (`MilnorK.degScale_quotEquiv_mk_tmul`). Within the formal rings: `MilnorKQ.quotEquiv`,
   `MilnorKQ.ker_proj`, `MilnorKQ.proj_surjective`, `MilnorKQ.relQuotEquiv` (for subgroups
   `H ≤ H'`, `MilnorKQ H'` is the quotient of `MilnorKQ H` by the ideal generated by `H'`).

## Why Theorems B, C, D are the paper's statements

Write `Φ_{K|k}` for the isomorphism of item 3 from the paper's `𝒦_*(K|k)` to
`MilnorKQ (constUnits k K)`, and `Φ_K` for that of item 2 (or item 3 with `H = ⊥`). Conjugation
by these isomorphisms, `φ ↦ Φ_{L|l} ∘ φ ∘ Φ_{K|k}⁻¹`, is a bijection between homomorphisms of
`ℚ`-algebras `𝒦_*(K|k) → 𝒦_*(L|l)` in the paper's sense and in the formal sense. Under it:

* `φ` is graded if and only if its conjugate satisfies `MilnorKQ.IsGraded`, because the
  isomorphisms identify the degree `n` parts (`MilnorK.grade_eq_span`; the degree `n` part of the
  paper's quotient ring is the image of `ℚ ⊗ K^M_n`);
* `φ₁` corresponds to `MilnorKQ.degOne`, and the class `[x] ∈ 𝒦_1(K|k)` of `x ∈ K^×` to
  `VOf.cls _ (.ofMul x)` (`MilnorK.quotEquiv_mk_one_tmul_ι`), so the injectivity of `φ₁` and the
  conditions (A3), (A4) on `ψ = φ₁ ∘ [·]` mean the same on both sides (in Theorem C, the
  composite `K^× → 𝒦_1(L) → V_{L|l₀}` is `VOf.proj bot_le ∘ degOne φ ∘ cls`);
* `τ_*` corresponds to `MilnorKQ.map τ` (`MilnorK.quotEquiv_mk_ratMap`,
  `MilnorK.ratEquiv_comp_ratMap`) and `r^deg` to `MilnorKQ.degScale r`
  (`MilnorK.degScale_quotEquiv_mk_tmul`, `MilnorK.degScale_ratEquiv_tmul`), so the conclusions
  `φ = r^deg ∘ τ_*` coincide.

Hence `theoremB` (stated for `MilnorKQ (constUnits k K) → MilnorKQ (constUnits l L)`), `theoremC`
and `theoremD` (stated for `MilnorKQ ⊥`) are the paper's Theorems B, C, D. (In Theorem D the
paper's conclusion `φ = r^deg ∘ τ_*` with `τ : K^i → L^i` implicitly identifies `𝒦_*(K)` with
`𝒦_*(K^i)`; the formal statement makes this explicit with `MilnorKQ.map (algebraMap K K^i)`.)
-/
